import type { LessonSection } from "../curriculum";

export const sections: readonly LessonSection[] = [
  {
    heading: null,
    blocks: [
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["How to read this"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"0.1. The two layers"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Every topic below is written twice. The first pass is the beginner layer: what the thing is, why it exists, in plain words. The second pass is the PhD layer: the exact mechanics, naming the fibers, lanes, phases and edge cases that explain the behavior you see. You can read only the first paragraphs of each section on a first pass and come back for the second. Nothing in the second layer is required to use React correctly; all of it is required to predict React correctly."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"0.2. Version baseline and honesty about status"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Statements are against React 18.x and 19.x, with 19.2 (released October 2025) as the most recent stable line this article is confident about. Where a feature is experimental or canary only, it says so. Where this article is unsure (mostly about things that were landing in late 2025), it says so explicitly rather than guessing. The single biggest way an article like this goes stale is API status, so section 6 is a compact status table you should re-verify against react.dev before relying on it."},
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Rendering fundamentals"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.1. Render versus commit: the two halves of every update"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. When something changes, React does two distinct jobs. First it figures out what the screen should look like by calling your component functions; that is rendering. Then it applies the resulting changes to the real DOM; that is committing. The split matters because the first job can be slow, paused, repeated or thrown away without anyone noticing, while the second job is the moment the user actually sees something, so it has to be fast and all at once. Keep this picture: rendering is React thinking, committing is React acting. Thinking can be interrupted; acting cannot."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The render phase walks a work in progress fiber tree, calling beginWork on each fiber on the way down and completeWork on the way up. beginWork invokes your function component (or the class render method), reconciles the returned elements against the existing children, and tags fibers with flags such as Placement, Update, ChildDeletion, Passive. completeWork creates or diffs host instances (DOM nodes) and bubbles subtreeFlags upward so the commit phase can skip subtrees with nothing to do. Nothing touches the live DOM during render; host instances for new nodes are created but detached. The commit phase is synchronous and has three sub phases run in order over the whole tree: before mutation (getSnapshotBeforeUpdate, and scheduling of passive effects), mutation (DOM insertions, updates, deletions, useLayoutEffect and ref cleanups of removed nodes, useInsertionEffect), and layout (useLayoutEffect bodies, ref attachment, componentDidMount and componentDidUpdate). After commit the root pointer is swapped so the work in progress tree becomes current. Passive effects (useEffect) are flushed afterwards, normally asynchronously after paint, but synchronously before the next discrete event is processed and synchronously at the end of a commit caused by a discrete event such as a click (this last behavior arrived in React 18 to keep discrete input handling consistent). The practical consequences: render phase code can run more than once per committed frame, or zero times for frames that get discarded; commit phase code runs exactly once per committed frame; layout effects block paint, passive effects do not."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.2. Reconciliation: diffing by position and key"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. React does not diff the DOM. It diffs the trees of elements your components return, and it uses two shortcuts to make that cheap: if two elements at the same position have different types, throw the old subtree away and build a new one; if they are lists, match children by key rather than by position. The key is a hint from you that says \"this is the same thing as before, even if it moved\". Without keys React assumes items at the same index are the same item, which is why editable lists keyed by index scramble their input state when you insert at the top."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. reconcileChildFibers takes the current child fibers and the new element array. For single children it compares key then type; a mismatch deletes and creates. For arrays it runs a two pass algorithm. Pass one walks old and new in lockstep while keys match, updating in place. On the first key mismatch it stops, builds a Map from key (or index when no key) to remaining old fibers, and for each remaining new element either reuses the matching old fiber (marking Placement if its old index is before the last placed index, which is how moves are detected using a lastPlacedIndex watermark) or creates a fresh fiber. Leftover old fibers are deleted. This is linear time at the cost of being non optimal for some move patterns, notably moving an item from the end to the front, which produces many Placement flags rather than one. A type change (including the same component name but a different function identity, which is what you get when you define a component inside another component's render) is a full unmount and remount with all state lost. Keys are scoped to siblings, not global, and changing a key is the sanctioned way to force a remount, which is sometimes exactly the right tool (resetting a form when the record id changes)."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.3. Fibers: what React actually holds in memory"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. For every component and DOM node on screen React keeps a small object called a fiber. It records what type it is, its props, its state (as a linked list of hooks), its parent, first child and next sibling, and some bookkeeping about pending work. Two trees exist: the current one that matches the screen, and a scratch copy being built for the next screen. Hooks are \"slots\" in a fiber's list, numbered by call order, which is the entire reason the rules of hooks exist: React finds your state by counting."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. A fiber is a plain object with fields including tag (FunctionComponent, HostComponent, SuspenseComponent, OffscreenComponent, ContextProvider and so on), type, key, stateNode, return, child, sibling, index, pendingProps, memoizedProps, memoizedState, updateQueue, flags, subtreeFlags, lanes, childLanes, and alternate. The alternate pointer links a current fiber to its work in progress twin; createWorkInProgress reuses the alternate object across renders so steady state allocates little. For function components, memoizedState is the head of a linked list of hook objects, each with memoizedState, baseState, baseQueue, queue and next. useState and useReducer share an update queue implemented as a circular linked list of update objects, each carrying a lane, the action, and an eagerState slot. lanes on a fiber records pending update priorities on that fiber; childLanes is the union for the subtree, which is what lets the render loop skip clean subtrees in constant time (bailoutOnAlreadyFinishedWork checks childLanes against the render lanes and clones children without descending when there is nothing to do). The tree is traversed iteratively using return and sibling pointers, not recursively, so the stack never holds the depth of your component tree, which is what made interruptible rendering physically possible in 2017 when the fiber architecture replaced the old recursive stack reconciler."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.4. Priorities and lanes: why some updates jump the queue"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Not every update is equally urgent. Typing into an input should be instant; re-filtering a ten thousand row table because of that keystroke can wait a few milliseconds. React 18 and later give updates priorities, let a high priority update interrupt lower priority work already in progress, and finish the interrupted work afterwards. You mostly influence this through a few APIs (startTransition, useDeferredValue) and through what kind of event the update originated from."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Priorities are lanes: bit positions in a 31 bit integer. The important ones, from highest to lowest: SyncLane (updates from discrete events such as click, keydown, input and anything in flushSync), InputContinuousLane (continuous events such as mousemove, scroll, drag), DefaultLane (updates outside any React event, for example from a setTimeout or a promise resolution, and the initial render), a block of TransitionLanes (startTransition work, with several lanes so independent transitions can be tracked separately), RetryLanes (re-attempts after a Suspense boundary's promise resolves), IdleLane and OffscreenLane. The event system assigns the lane: getCurrentUpdatePriority is set while React dispatches an event, and setState reads it via requestUpdateLane. Rendering picks the highest priority set of pending lanes on the root (getNextLanes) and renders only updates in those lanes; updates in lower lanes are skipped in that pass and kept in the hook's baseQueue so that later renders replay them in order. This skipping is why a single hook can produce a temporarily \"wrong\" intermediate state in a high priority render and the correct state in the follow up render; React preserves update order per hook by rebasing. Lanes can be entangled: if two transitions touch the same hook queue, React forces them to render together so updates never apply out of order. Starvation is bounded: each lane records an expiration time and once expired its work is rendered synchronously regardless of what else is pending. The Scheduler package, which is separate from the reconciler, hosts the actual cooperative loop. It posts a macrotask through MessageChannel, works until shouldYield reports that the 5 millisecond frame budget is spent (or that there is pending input, where the browser supports isInputPending), then yields and reposts. Sync lane work does not go through this loop; it is flushed in a microtask at the end of the current event so that discrete input is never time sliced."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.5. Batching: many setState calls, one render"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. If you call setState three times in a row, React does not render three times. It collects the updates and renders once with all of them applied. Before React 18 this only worked inside React event handlers; a setTimeout or a fetch callback got one render per call. Since React 18, with createRoot, it works everywhere. If you really need the DOM updated synchronously between two updates, flushSync exists, and you should feel slightly dirty using it."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. dispatchSetState enqueues an update on the hook's queue and calls scheduleUpdateOnFiber, which marks lanes from the fiber up to the root (markUpdateLaneFromFiberToRoot) and calls ensureRootIsScheduled. That function is idempotent per root per priority: it checks whether a callback at the right priority is already scheduled and does nothing if so. For SyncLane it queues a microtask (scheduleMicrotask) to flushSyncCallbacks; for other lanes it schedules a Scheduler task. Because all three setState calls in your handler land before the microtask or task runs, they share one render. Automatic batching in 18 is therefore not a special feature; it fell out of routing all updates through ensureRootIsScheduled instead of relying on an isBatchingUpdates flag set only by React's event wrapper. flushSync raises the update priority to SyncLane and flushes the sync queue immediately on exit; inside a render or effect it warns. One subtle consequence: an update scheduled inside useLayoutEffect or a class componentDidMount is also SyncLane and flushes before the browser paints, which is how layout measurement then repaint patterns avoid flicker."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.6. Eager state and the bail outs that make React cheaper than it looks"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. If you set state to the same value it already has, React usually does nothing at all, not even re-run your component. React also skips re-rendering children whose props and context have not changed when it can prove that cheaply. These shortcuts are why \"React re-renders everything\" is not quite true, and also why \"React re-renders nothing it doesn't have to\" is not quite true either."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. In dispatchSetState, if the fiber has no pending work (fiber.lanes and alternate.lanes are both NoLanes), React computes the new state eagerly by running the reducer right there, stores it in update.eagerState, and compares with Object.is to the current state. If equal, it enqueues the update as a no op without scheduling a render (enqueueConcurrentHookUpdateAndEagerlyBailout). If the fiber already has pending updates the eager path is skipped because the base state might differ once earlier updates are applied. There is a second, weaker bail out during render: if a component renders and its new state equals the old state, React can call bailoutHooks and skip its children, but the component function itself has already been invoked once. That is the source of the folk observation \"setting the same state sometimes re-renders once and then stops\". A third bail out is props based: in beginWork, if oldProps equals newProps by reference, no context changed, and the fiber has no pending lanes, React skips the component entirely. Note the reference comparison: a parent re-rendering produces fresh props objects for every child element, so this bail out essentially never fires for children of a re-rendering parent unless something (React.memo, or passing children as a prop from a non re-rendering ancestor) preserves the element identity. That is the real mechanism behind both memo and the \"lift the expensive child into children\" trick."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"1.7. Render purity: the contract that makes everything else legal"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Your component function must be pure during rendering: same props, state and context in, same elements out, and no side effects while computing. React relies on this to call your function more than once, to throw away a render it no longer needs, and to render things in the background. Side effects belong in event handlers or effects. In development, StrictMode deliberately calls your component twice (and mounts, unmounts, remounts effects) to shake out impurities; it is not a bug and it does not happen in production."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Strict Mode double invocation in development covers: function component bodies, useState and useReducer initializer functions and reducer invocations, useMemo callbacks, class constructors, render, shouldComponentUpdate and getDerivedStateFromProps, and, since React 19, ref callback functions. The second invocation's result is the one used. React 18 dimmed or suppressed console output from the second call in DevTools; React 19 removed that suppression, so you see two logs. Effects get a mount, cleanup, mount cycle on first mount in development (React 18 and later) to simulate the reusable state that Activity and Fast Refresh require. What purity buys React concretely: a render may be interrupted at any fiber boundary by higher priority work and discarded (the work in progress tree is simply abandoned and rebuilt from current); a transition render may be computed while the old UI stays on screen; a Suspense boundary may render its children, hit a thrown promise, and throw away the partial subtree; the compiler (React Compiler, 1.0 as of October 2025) may memoize values based on the assumption that render has no observable effects. Impure renders break under exactly these conditions and nowhere else, which is why they \"work on my machine\" and fail under load."},
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Concurrent features"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.1. startTransition and useTransition: mark work as interruptible"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Wrap a state update in startTransition and you tell React \"this one can wait, and if something more urgent comes in, pause or restart it\". The classic example is a search box: the input value updates normally so typing feels instant, the filtered results update in a transition so a slow filter never blocks keystrokes. useTransition additionally gives you an isPending flag so you can dim the stale results. In React 19 the callback can be async; React tracks the pending state across awaits and this is the foundation of Actions and form submission."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. startTransition sets a module level transition marker (ReactCurrentBatchConfig.transition) for the duration of the callback, so every setState inside receives a TransitionLane from requestUpdateLane. Transition renders go through the concurrent work loop with time slicing, can be interrupted by Sync or InputContinuous work, and critically interact with Suspense differently: if a transition render suspends inside a Suspense boundary that is already showing content, React does not commit the fallback; it keeps the old UI, keeps isPending true, and waits for the promise. If the suspending boundary has never shown content it still shows its fallback. This \"avoid hiding already revealed content\" behavior is the main thing people mean by \"transitions make Suspense feel good\". React 19 extended the marker across async boundaries: when the async callback awaits, React 19 ensures updates in the continuation are still treated as transitions and keeps isPending until the returned promise settles, and multiple transitions can be in flight. React 18 did not do this; updates after an await in 18 were plain default lane updates and isPending dropped early. In 19 the same machinery backs form actions: a form with an action prop runs the action inside a transition, useFormStatus reads its pending state, and useActionState gives you the returned value plus pending flag."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.2. useDeferredValue: a transition for values you don't control"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Sometimes you cannot wrap the update in startTransition because the value comes from a parent or a prop. useDeferredValue takes any value and gives you a copy that lags behind under load: React first re-renders with the old deferred value (fast, because the expensive subtree sees no change) and then renders again in the background with the new one. Pass the deferred value to the expensive component, optionally wrapped in memo so the first fast render actually skips it."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The hook stores the previous value; when the incoming value differs, it returns the previous value and schedules a deferred re-render of the component in a TransitionLane (updateDeferredValueImpl marks the fiber and requests a transition lane). In that follow up render it returns the new value. Because it is \"just\" a transition update on this fiber, the deferred render is interruptible and does not trigger fallbacks of already visible Suspense boundaries; if the deferred value causes a child to suspend, the stale content stays and no fallback flashes. React 19 added an optional initialValue argument used for the very first render (useful during hydration or initial mount to render something cheap first). The important mental correction: useDeferredValue does not debounce or throttle; on a fast machine with cheap children both renders may complete within one frame, and that is fine, because its job is to let urgent work preempt, not to delay for its own sake. Pairing with memo is not optional for performance: without memo the \"fast\" first render still re-renders the expensive child with the unchanged value, it just does so with the same props objects created fresh, which defeats the bail out from 1.6."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.3. Suspense: declaring loading states, and the exact reveal rules"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Suspense lets a component say \"I'm not ready\" and lets a boundary higher up show a fallback until it is. React.lazy uses it for code splitting; data libraries and the use hook use it for data. The rule people most often get wrong is how nested boundaries reveal: content inside an inner boundary cannot appear before its outer boundary has revealed, because it isn't mounted until then; sibling boundaries reveal independently in whatever order their data arrives. If you want siblings to reveal in a fixed order you need SuspenseList, and SuspenseList is still experimental as of React 19.2, so in stable React you get independent reveal, full stop."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. A component suspends by throwing a thenable (promise). The work loop catches it (throwException), finds the nearest SuspenseComponent fiber up the return path, marks it ShouldCapture, and unwinds to it. The boundary then renders its fallback; the primary children subtree is kept as an OffscreenComponent fiber in hidden mode so that state in already mounted children is preserved rather than destroyed. React attaches a ping listener to the thenable; when it resolves, pingSuspendedRoot schedules a RetryLane render of that boundary. Reveal rules follow from this: an inner boundary's primary children are not rendered until the outer boundary's primary tree is being rendered, so nesting imposes an outer to inner order. Siblings each carry their own ping, so they reveal when ready, independently. Two timing refinements change what you observe. First, React throttles successive reveals: after a boundary commits revealed content, additional reveals in the next roughly 300 milliseconds are batched to avoid a cascade of layout shifts (FALLBACK_THROTTLE_MS). Second, React 19 changed sibling pre-rendering: when a component suspends during a render that would show a fallback, React 19 commits the fallback immediately instead of continuing to render the remaining siblings first, then schedules a background \"prewarm\" render of the suspended siblings so their lazy requests start. React 18 kept rendering siblings before committing the fallback, which started their fetches in parallel but delayed the fallback. The 19 behavior improves time to fallback but means render time data fetching initiated by siblings is serialized behind the first suspension unless you preload or hoist those fetches; the React team's guidance is to kick off data requests before render (in a router loader, a server component, or via preload APIs), which is also the general cure for waterfalls in section 3.6. Inside a transition, a boundary that has already revealed content stays revealed (the transition waits) and only never revealed boundaries show fallbacks. Error boundaries and Suspense boundaries use the same unwind machinery; the difference is only what is thrown."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.4. useSyncExternalStore and tearing: the one hook that refuses to be concurrent"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Concurrent rendering means a render can pause partway through. If during that pause a value outside React (a Redux store, window.innerWidth, a WebSocket cache) changes, the top half of your tree could be rendered with the old value and the bottom half with the new one. That is tearing: one screen, two realities. useSyncExternalStore is how you read outside data safely. You give it a subscribe function and a getSnapshot function; React promises the whole tree sees one consistent snapshot per commit, and if the store changes mid render React falls back to rendering synchronously so nothing can slip in between."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The hook reads getSnapshot during render and stores the value in hook state together with the getSnapshot function. It pushes a store consistency check onto the root (pushStoreConsistencyCheck) for every uSES call in a concurrent render. At the end of the render phase, before commit, React re-invokes each getSnapshot and compares with Object.is against what was rendered; if any differs (the store changed while rendering was yielded), the render is discarded and re-run synchronously (renderRootSync) so there is no yield window in which tearing could happen. Updates from the store's subscribe callback are forced to SyncLane (forceStoreRerender), which is why a store update cannot be deferred by startTransition even if you dispatch it inside one; the pending transition is cut short, old UI is not preserved, and fallbacks may show. This is the deliberate design trade: external stores get consistency and lose participation in transitions. getSnapshot must return a referentially stable value when nothing changed; returning a new object every call causes an infinite re-render loop with a dev warning. The optional third argument getServerSnapshot supplies the value used on the server and during hydration so that the hydrated render matches the server HTML; without it you get a hydration mismatch or an error. Earlier library patterns such as subscribe in useEffect plus setState were tear prone because the subscription attached after the first render completed, leaving a gap, and because their setState updates could be assigned a lower lane and interleave."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.5. useId: stable identifiers across server and client"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. If you need an id for an aria attribute or a label's htmlFor, generate it with useId, not Math.random and not a counter. Random ids differ between server and client and break hydration; counters break when components render in different orders or when hydration happens out of order. useId derives the id from the component's position in the tree, so both sides agree."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The id is a base 32 encoding of the path from the root to the component, built from a tree id maintained on the stack during render (pushTreeId tracks fork indices as React enters each child of a multi child parent and each useId call within a component increments a local counter). Because it is positional, it is stable regardless of hydration order, which selective hydration requires. On the client without SSR a simpler global counter is used. The identifierPrefix option on createRoot and hydrateRoot namespaces ids when multiple React roots coexist on one page. The ids contain colon characters by design (in 19 they switched to a different delimiter in some builds; treat them as opaque strings) so they cannot be used as CSS selectors without escaping; use them for attributes, not for querySelector."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.6. useOptimistic: show the hoped for result, revert on failure"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. When the user clicks \"like\", you want the heart to fill instantly even though the server round trip takes 300 milliseconds. useOptimistic lets you hold a temporary optimistic version of some state while an action is in flight; when the action finishes (or fails) React snaps back to the real value. Stable since React 19."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. useOptimistic(passthroughState, reducer) returns the optimistic state and an addOptimistic dispatcher. Internally it is a state hook whose updates are only applied during a transition's lifetime: addOptimistic must be called inside a transition or action (outside one React warns and the update is applied and immediately dropped). React renders the optimistic value in the transition lane; when the transition completes the hook's optimistic updates are discarded and the passthrough value, which by then should reflect the server result, is shown. If the action throws, the same discard happens, so the UI reverts without you writing revert code. It composes with useActionState and form actions, and it is the official replacement for libraries' bespoke optimistic update layers in simple cases."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.7. use: read a promise or a context without hook rules"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. use is a function, not quite a hook: you call use(promise) to read a promise's result (suspending until it resolves) or use(Context) to read context, and unlike hooks you may call it inside conditions and loops. Stable since React 19. The catch: the promise must be stable across renders. Creating a new promise in render on each call means React never sees it resolve from the same identity and you get an endless suspend loop plus a warning. In practice promises come from a server component (passed as a prop), a cache, or a data library."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. use(thenable) checks the thenable's status field; React itself writes status, value and reason onto the thenable the first time it sees it (trackUsedThenable) so that a cached promise becomes synchronously readable on subsequent renders. If status is fulfilled it returns value; if rejected it throws reason so an error boundary can catch it; otherwise it throws a SuspenseException that the work loop treats like a thrown promise. Because React tracks thenables per fiber in a thenable state array indexed by call order during that render pass, calling use in a conditional is safe: the index is reset per attempt and the array is keyed by render, not by committed hook slot. use(Context) is readContext under the hood with the same propagation as useContext but permitted after early returns. On the server in React Server Components, use on a promise suspends the server render and streams. A promise created inside render on the client is not cached between attempts, which is the warning you see; the fix is to create it in a parent that does not re-render per attempt, in an event, in a loader, or memoize it keyed by inputs."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.8. Streaming SSR and selective hydration: shipping HTML in pieces"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Old server rendering produced one big HTML string after all data was ready, then the client downloaded all JavaScript, then ran it over the whole page before anything was interactive. React 18 streams: the server sends the shell immediately, sends each Suspense boundary's HTML when its data is ready (with inline script tags that swap it into place), and the client hydrates boundaries one at a time, prioritizing the one the user is trying to interact with. The user sees something early and can click early."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. renderToPipeableStream (Node) and renderToReadableStream (web streams) run the Fizz server renderer. Suspense boundaries that suspend emit their fallback HTML in the initial flush with a template placeholder and a boundary id; when the data resolves the server emits a hidden div with the content plus a small inline script calling $RC (React Complete) that moves the nodes into place. Hydration on the client is hydrateRoot; each Suspense boundary becomes a unit of hydration (a dehydrated SuspenseComponent fiber). Hydration proceeds in idle priority by default, boundary by boundary, outermost first. When the user interacts with a dehydrated boundary, React's event system (which attaches listeners at the root) records the event, upgrades that boundary's hydration to the event's priority (attemptSynchronousHydration for discrete events, continuous for others), hydrates that subtree first, then replays the event. Hydration must produce the same tree as the server HTML; mismatches in 18 patched silently with a warning for text, and in 19 any mismatch inside a boundary causes a client render of that boundary with a console diff showing exactly where the trees diverged. React 19.2 added server side batching of Suspense boundary reveals (brief throttling, similar in spirit to the client throttle) to reduce layout thrash from many small flushes, plus prerender and resume APIs enabling partial pre-rendering, where a static shell is rendered at build time and the remainder resumed at request time. Treat the 19.2 details as recently landed and worth re-checking."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"2.9. Activity: keep a subtree alive but hidden"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Tabs, modals and background routes have a tension: unmount them and you lose their state and scroll position; keep them mounted and they keep rendering and running effects. Activity lets you mark a subtree as hidden: it is removed from layout and accessibility, its effects are cleaned up, its state is preserved, and React keeps it pre-rendered at low priority so switching back is instant. This component was called Offscreen, then unstable_Activity, and shipped as stable Activity in React 19.2 in October 2025 with mode set to visible or hidden. If you are on 19.1 or earlier it is not available in stable builds."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Activity is the user facing name for the OffscreenComponent fiber that Suspense already used internally to park suspended primary children. In hidden mode React renders the subtree in OffscreenLane (the lowest priority, run only when the root is otherwise idle), sets display none on host nodes via a style override during the mutation phase, and in commit runs the subtree's layout and passive effect cleanups (disappearLayoutEffects, disconnectPassiveEffect) while keeping fibers and hook state intact. Switching to visible reconnects effects (reappearLayoutEffects) in creation order, which is why Strict Mode's double mount simulation exists: your effects must survive cleanup and re-run with preserved state. Updates inside a hidden Activity subtree are deferred to OffscreenLane so they never compete with visible work. A planned but not shipped (as of this article's confidence) extension is a third mode for pre-rendering not yet shown content; verify current docs."},
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Performance"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.1. Why a component re-renders: the complete list"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. A component re-renders when its own state changes, when its parent re-renders (regardless of whether its props changed), when a context it reads changes, or when a hook it uses forces an update (uSES, a data library). That is the full list. \"Props changed\" is not on it; props changing is a symptom of the parent re-rendering, and props not changing does not prevent a re-render unless memo is involved. Most surprising re-renders come from the parent case: a state change at the top of the tree re-renders everything below that is not memoized."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The render loop descends into a fiber when any of these hold in beginWork: the fiber's lanes intersect the render lanes (own update), didReceiveUpdate is set because oldProps !== newProps (parent rendered and produced new element props), or a context dependency changed (propagateContextChange walked the subtree when the provider's value failed Object.is and scheduled the consumer fibers by marking their lanes). Otherwise bailoutOnAlreadyFinishedWork clones children and only descends if childLanes intersect render lanes. Note the Object.is on provider value: passing an inline object as value means every provider render invalidates all consumers. Note also that class components with shouldComponentUpdate or PureComponent and function components in memo are the only places where the props comparison is by shallow equality instead of reference."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.2. memo, useMemo, useCallback: when they help"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. memo wraps a component so that it skips re-rendering when its props are shallowly equal to last time. useMemo caches a computed value between renders; useCallback caches a function. They help in exactly two situations: when a child is genuinely expensive to render and its props are stable, and when a value must be referentially stable because something downstream compares by reference (a memo child, an effect dependency array, a context value). Outside those two situations they do nothing useful."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. memo creates a MemoComponent (or SimpleMemoComponent when there is no custom compare and no defaultProps) whose beginWork compares pendingProps against memoizedProps with shallowEqual and bails out when equal and no own lanes are pending. The comparison walks own enumerable keys and uses Object.is per key, so an inline object, array, arrow function or JSX element as a prop defeats it on every render. useMemo and useCallback store the value plus the dependency array in hook state and on each render compare dependencies with areHookInputsEqual (Object.is per slot); on a match they return the cached value without invoking the factory. The first invocation always runs. In Strict Mode development, useMemo factories run twice on mount (and on dependency change in 19) and the second result is kept. The React Compiler automates all of this by inserting cache slots keyed on the actual reactive inputs it infers from the code, which is why its adoption guidance is to delete manual memoization gradually rather than keep both; manual memo and compiler memo coexist but the manual version becomes dead weight."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.3. When memoization hurts, and why memo everything loses"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Every memo, useMemo and useCallback costs something: it stores the previous props or deps and compares them on every render. If the comparison rarely succeeds (because something in the props changes each time anyway) you pay the comparison and still re-render. If the component is cheap, the comparison can cost more than the render it would have skipped. Memoizing everything also makes code harder to read and makes bugs where a stale value sticks around because a dependency was forgotten. The right move is to measure first (section 3.5), fix the one or two components that matter, and otherwise write plain code."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The cost model: a bailed out memo component still has a fiber visited (beginWork is called), its props are shallow compared, and its children are cloned. A failed memo comparison adds that work to a full render. Dependency arrays allocate an array per render and compare per slot. None of this is large individually, but memo on hundreds of leaf components across a list adds measurable overhead in profiles, and it is overhead on every render including the common case where the list rerenders because an item changed and the item's props did change. Memoization is also only as good as its weakest link: one unmemoized object prop (a style literal, an inline handler, a children element) resets the chain, and in a tree where memo is applied reflexively, the chain is almost always broken somewhere, yielding full cost with zero benefit. The structural alternatives are usually better: move state down to where it is used (section 4.1) so fewer components are in the re-render path; pass expensive subtrees as children from a component that does not re-render, which keeps their element identity stable and triggers the props reference bail out in 1.6 with no memo at all; split a context into frequently and rarely changing halves; virtualize long lists so the number of fibers is bounded by viewport. Where memoization is correct, it is correct for a reason you can name in a sentence; if you cannot name it, delete it, and if you can adopt the compiler, let it make the call per expression."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.4. Keys and lazy, briefly, as performance tools"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Keys are mostly about correctness, but they are also performance: a stable key turns an insert at the top of a list from \"re-render and re-patch every row\" into \"create one row and move the rest\". A changed key is the cheapest way to reset a subtree. React.lazy splits code so the initial bundle is smaller and ties naturally into Suspense for the loading state; its cost is a network round trip at the moment the component is first needed, which is why routers preload lazy chunks on hover or link visibility."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Index keys on reorderable lists cause fibers to be reused for the wrong data: state, uncontrolled input values and effects stay attached to the position while content shifts underneath. Random keys generated in render cause a full unmount and remount of every item each render. lazy returns a special LazyComponent type whose payload records a pending, resolved or rejected status; the first render calls the factory, throws the resulting promise to suspend, and subsequent renders read the resolved module synchronously. lazy chunks fetched during render are a form of waterfall (section 3.6); preloading by calling the import function early, before the component is rendered, converts them to parallel."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.5. Profiler and measuring before fixing"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. React DevTools has a Profiler tab that records each commit, shows which components rendered and why (when \"record why each component rendered\" is enabled), and how long each took. There is also a Profiler component you can wrap around a subtree in code to collect timings programmatically. Use these before adding any memo. The usual finding is that two or three components account for almost all the time and the rest is noise."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The Profiler component's onRender callback receives id, phase (mount, update, nested-update), actualDuration (time rendering the subtree this commit, including memoized bail outs which show near zero), baseDuration (estimated full render without memoization, useful as the \"what did memo save me\" number), startTime and commitTime. Profiling builds of React (react-dom/profiling) add timing fields to fibers that the production build omits, so production numbers require the profiling bundle alias. React 19.2 added Performance tracks to the browser Performance panel showing scheduler lanes and component render timings in the standard devtools timeline, which is now the best place to see priority behavior and time slicing with real frames. Measure interaction latency (INP) rather than render counts; a component rendering ten times in 2 milliseconds is not a problem, a component rendering once in 120 milliseconds on a click is."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.6. Data waterfalls"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. A waterfall is when request two cannot start until request one finishes, not because it needs its result but because of where the code lives: the child that needs data cannot run its fetch until the parent's fetch resolves and the child renders. Three or four of these in a chain turn a 200 millisecond page into a second. The cure is always the same: start fetches as early as possible and in parallel, at the route or server level, and let components read results rather than initiate requests."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Render initiated fetching (fetch in useEffect, or a suspending read in render whose fetch starts on first call) couples request start to render order and depth, and in React 19 the immediate fallback commit (2.3) serializes sibling render initiated fetches behind the first suspension until the prewarm pass runs. Routers with loaders, server components that fetch on the server where latency to data is low, the preload family in react-dom (preload, preinit, prefetchDNS, preconnect, stable in 19) for assets, and cache keyed by inputs so that an early initiated request is reused by the component that later reads it, all decouple request start from render. On the server, RSC turns a client side waterfall into a server side one, which is still a waterfall but often an order of magnitude cheaper per hop; the structural fix remains parallelizing independent fetches with Promise.all or by starting them before awaiting any of them."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"3.7. Hydration cost and what reduces it"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Hydration is React rendering your whole component tree in the browser just to attach event handlers to HTML that already exists. For a large page that means downloading and executing a lot of JavaScript to produce a result the user already sees. Selective hydration spreads it out and prioritizes it; server components remove components from the client bundle entirely so there is less to hydrate; Activity and lazy keep non visible parts out of the initial pass."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Hydration runs the full render phase against a dehydrated fiber tree, walking DOM nodes in step with fibers (tryToClaimNextHydratableInstance) and attaching stateNode pointers instead of creating nodes; it still executes every component function and every hook. Its cost scales with tree size and component logic, not with DOM size. React Server Components never ship to the client; their output arrives as a serialized element tree (the RSC payload) that the client reconciles like any other elements, so the hydration cost for a server component subtree is just reconciliation of static elements, with no component code executed on the client. The remaining client components hydrate per Suspense boundary at idle priority unless interaction upgrades them. Common cost multipliers: huge lists rendered server side with client components per row (virtualize or make rows server components), expensive useMemo initializers that run again during hydration, and effects that fire on hydration and trigger an immediate re-render of the entire page (a theme or auth check at the root that sets state on mount re-renders everything twice)."},
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["State management"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.1. Colocation and lifting"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Put state in the lowest component that needs it. If two siblings need it, lift it to their nearest common parent. That is the whole algorithm, and most state management pain comes from violating one half of it: state lifted too high (every keystroke re-renders the page) or state kept too low (two components disagree about the same fact). Lifting all the way to a global store should be the last resort, not the first."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Colocation is a performance decision because of 3.1: the re-render frontier of a state change is the owning component's subtree. Moving state from a page component to a leaf shrinks the frontier from thousands of fibers to one. Lifting is a correctness decision: one source of truth means no synchronization code. The standard compromise when state must be high but the subtree is wide is the children trick: the stateful component takes the wide subtree as a children prop from its parent, so when it re-renders, the children elements are the same objects and bail out by reference. This is why layout components that own scroll or theme state and wrap the entire app are not automatically a performance disaster."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.2. Context: what it costs and what it is for"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Context passes a value down the tree without threading props. Every component that reads it re-renders when the value changes, and it cannot subscribe to only part of the value. That makes context perfect for things that change rarely (theme, locale, current user, a stable store instance) and poor for things that change often (form state, mouse position, a frequently updated cache). The usual fixes are to split one context into several by change frequency, to memoize the value object, or to put a store instance in context and subscribe to slices with uSES."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. When a provider's value fails Object.is against the previous, propagateContextChange walks the provider's subtree looking for fibers whose dependencies list includes this context and marks their lanes (and the childLanes up to the provider). This walk is linear in subtree size and happens on every value change, even before any consumer renders. Consumers then re-render in full; there is no selector, and the experimental unstable_useContextSelector never shipped. React 19 lets you render the context object itself as the provider (Context rather than Context.Provider) which is sugar, not a semantic change. The store in context pattern (Redux, Zustand with a context bound store) works because the context value is the store object which never changes identity; updates flow through uSES subscriptions to only the components whose selected slice changed, bypassing propagateContextChange entirely."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.3. Reducers: transitions as data"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. useReducer is useState where you describe changes as actions and write one function that says what each action does to the state. It pays off when several fields change together, when the next state depends on the previous in non trivial ways, or when you want to test the logic without rendering. It is the same idea Redux is built on, at component scale."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. useState is implemented as useReducer with basicStateReducer; the queues, lanes, eager state and rebasing in 1.4 and 1.6 apply identically. One difference: with useReducer the eager state optimization compares reducer output, and because reducers can be redefined per render (closing over props), React re-runs queued actions with the latest reducer during render rather than trusting the eagerly computed value when the reducer identity changed. Dispatch identity is stable across renders, so it can be passed down without useCallback. Reducers make the update order guarantees in 1.4 easy to reason about: a queue of actions is replayed in order against a base state, which is exactly what rebasing skipped updates needs."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.4. Derived versus duplicated state"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. If something can be computed from existing state and props, compute it during render; do not store it in state and try to keep it in sync. A filtered list is derived from the list and the filter; a full name is derived from first and last; whether a form is valid is derived from its fields. Storing derived values creates the possibility of them being wrong and the obligation to update them in every place the source changes. useMemo is for when the derivation is expensive, not for making derived state \"real\"."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. The anti pattern useEffect that calls setState to mirror a prop or compute a value produces two commits per change (the first with stale derived state, the second corrected), is visible as flicker under layout sensitive UI, and interacts badly with transitions because the effect runs after commit and its setState is a default lane update outside the transition. When a derivation must reset on a prop change (a draft that follows a selected id), the sanctioned patterns are a key on the component so it remounts, or the \"adjust state during render\" pattern where you compare the prop to a stored previous value and call setState conditionally during render, which React handles by re-rendering immediately before committing (it loops in renderWithHooks up to 25 times). getDerivedStateFromProps is the class era equivalent. The constraint in all cases is that derived state has no independent authority; the moment it does, it is a second source of truth and you are back to synchronization."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.5. External stores and the shape of the three big models"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Redux, Zustand and Jotai are three answers to \"where does shared state live and how do components subscribe\". Redux is one big object updated by reducers, read through selectors; its strength is a strict, auditable data flow and tooling, its cost is ceremony (much reduced by Redux Toolkit). Zustand is a small store you define with a create function, read with selectors, mutate with set; minimal ceremony, same subscription model. Jotai is atoms, tiny pieces of state that components subscribe to individually and that can derive from each other; its strength is fine grained updates without selectors. All three use useSyncExternalStore under the hood in current versions, which is why they are safe under concurrent rendering and also why their updates cannot be transitions (see 5.1)."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Redux's useSelector calls uSES with a subscribe to the store and a getSnapshot that runs the selector; selector results are compared with Object.is (or shallowEqual if passed), and a selector returning a new object each call triggers the uSES loop warning and re-renders every dispatch. React Redux adds a stability check in development that runs selectors twice. Zustand's useStore is the same call with the selector applied to the store's state; its equality defaults to Object.is with useShallow as the opt in. Jotai keeps a store of atom values with a dependency graph; useAtomValue subscribes to one atom via uSES, derived atoms recompute when dependencies change and only notify if their value changed, giving selector free granularity at the cost of state being distributed across many atoms rather than one inspectable object. Across all three, the per component cost is a subscription plus a getSnapshot call per render plus the consistency check at commit; the global cost is that every store update notifies every subscriber, which each run their selector, which is linear in subscriber count per update regardless of how many actually change (Jotai's graph reduces this to subscribers of affected atoms). Choosing among them is mostly about team conventions and devtools; the runtime models have converged."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.6. Server cache versus client state"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Most of what used to go into global stores was not state at all; it was a cache of server data. Fetched lists, user profiles, search results: the server owns them, your copy is stale the moment it arrives, and what you actually need is fetching, caching, deduplication, background refresh and invalidation. Libraries like TanStack Query and SWR do exactly that, and React Server Components move the whole category to the server. What remains as true client state is small: form drafts, open menus, selected tab, optimistic flags. Separating the two makes both simpler."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Server cache libraries implement a normalized or key based cache with stale while revalidate semantics, request deduplication by key, structural sharing so unchanged data keeps identity (preserving memo bail outs), and subscription via uSES or equivalent. Their retry, focus refetch and garbage collection behavior is policy that a Redux slice would have to reimplement. RSC inverts the model: components that read server data run on the server, their output is streamed, and the client cache becomes the router's cache of RSC payloads keyed by URL; client state is whatever client components hold. The cache function in React 19 memoizes per server request so repeated calls with the same arguments in one render dedupe without a client library. In both models the invariant to keep: server data is read, never written locally except through an optimistic layer that is explicitly temporary (useOptimistic or the library's optimistic update), and the server response is the authority that overwrites it."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"4.7. Minimal state principles, collected"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Beginner layer. Keep only what cannot be computed. Keep it as low as possible and as high as necessary. Prefer one source of truth to synchronization. Treat server data as a cache, not state. Reach for context for stable values, for a store for frequently updated shared values, for a reducer when updates are structured. Measure before memoizing. Most applications that follow these need no state library at all beyond a data fetching one."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"PhD layer. Each principle maps to a mechanism: minimal state minimizes the number of update queues that can desynchronize and the number of effects that exist to resynchronize them; colocation minimizes re-render frontier per update (3.1); single source of truth avoids the double commit of effect driven mirroring (4.4); server data as cache moves consistency to a layer built for stale while revalidate rather than to reducers; stable context values avoid propagateContextChange churn (4.2); stores for hot values use uSES's targeted subscriptions instead of context's broadcast; reducers make rebasing of skipped updates (1.4) mechanical. The principles are not style; they are the configurations under which React's bail outs fire and its concurrency guarantees hold."},
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["Synthesis: how the pieces constrain each other"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"5.1. Tearing reshaped external stores, and the price was transitions"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Concurrent rendering created tearing as a problem, uSES solved it by forcing consistency, and the solution has a cost that reshapes state architecture: external store updates are synchronous and cannot participate in transitions. If a search term lives in Zustand and results are derived from it, startTransition around the store set does nothing useful; the update is Sync, the expensive results re-render blocks input, and Suspense boundaries show fallbacks instead of holding old content. If the same search term lives in useState, the transition works as advertised. The consequence is a split that did not exist in 2019: state that benefits from transitions (anything driving expensive or suspending UI in response to user input) wants to be React owned state, while state that needs to be read consistently from many places and updated from outside React (sockets, caches, cross cutting app state) wants to be an external store. Jotai's atoms and Redux's slices both inherit the sync constraint. Server cache libraries sidestep part of it: their data arrives from the network, not from user input, so Sync updates on arrival are rarely the bottleneck, and their suspense integrations use the use or throw promise path which is transition friendly on the read side. Design rule: user input that drives heavy UI goes through useState or useReducer, possibly mirrored outward via an effect or action; everything else may live outside."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"5.2. useDeferredValue plus fetching: the suspending read pattern"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"Combine 2.2, 2.3 and 2.7 and you get the idiom that replaced isLoading flags. A component reads data with use(promise) or a library's suspense hook, keyed on a query. The input updates a state value immediately; the component passes useDeferredValue(query) to the data read. When the query changes, the first render uses the old deferred query, finds the cached resolved data, and renders without suspending; the second, deferred render uses the new query, suspends on the new promise, and because it is a transition lane render, React holds the old results on screen (no fallback) until the promise resolves, with isPending or a comparison of query versus deferred query available to dim the stale results. No loading boolean, no race condition handling (a superseded deferred render is simply discarded), no effect. The constraints that make it work: the promise must be cached by query so the same thenable is seen across attempts; the fetch should start before or at the first suspending read, not in an effect; the data component should be memoized or cheap so the first fast render is actually fast. React 19's immediate fallback commit does not interfere because in a transition no fallback is committed for revealed boundaries."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"5.3. Principles that fall out"},
      {"kind":"p","text":"First, purity is not a style preference; it is the precondition for interruption, discarding, pre-rendering (Activity), transitions, and the compiler, and impurity fails precisely under the conditions those features introduce. Second, priority is assigned by origin: where an update comes from (discrete event, continuous event, transition, external store, timeout) determines its lane, and therefore whether it can be deferred, whether it preserves old UI, and whether it blocks input; architecture decides origin, so architecture decides latency. Third, start work early and read late: fetches, imports and preloads initiated before render turn waterfalls into parallel requests and make the suspending read pattern viable; components should consume readiness, not create it. Fourth, bail outs are reference based, so identity is the currency of performance: stable element identity (children trick), stable context value identity, stable snapshot identity (uSES), stable promise identity (use); memoization is one way to buy identity and structure is usually a cheaper one. Fifth, Suspense boundaries are the unit of everything asynchronous: streaming chunks, hydration units, transition hold points, fallback scopes; placing them is a product decision about what can appear independently, and nesting them encodes the only reveal order guarantee you get without SuspenseList. Sixth, consistency has a cost and you choose where to pay it: uSES pays with synchrony, context pays with broadcast re-renders, derived state pays with recomputation, duplicated state pays with synchronization bugs; pick the payment that fits the update frequency. Seventh, the long arc of React's history is moving decisions out of components: fiber moved scheduling out of your render, hooks moved lifecycle out of classes, Suspense moved loading state out of booleans, RSC moved data access out of the client, and the compiler moves memoization out of your hands; the state management that survives this arc is the small residue of truly client, truly interactive, truly ephemeral state."},
      {"kind":"list","ordered":true,"items":["API status table, as of React 19.2"]},
      {"kind":"p","text":"6.1. Stable in React 18 and later createRoot and hydrateRoot; automatic batching; startTransition and useTransition; useDeferredValue; useSyncExternalStore; useId; useInsertionEffect; Suspense for lazy and for data via libraries or thrown promises; streaming SSR with renderToPipeableStream and renderToReadableStream; selective hydration; Strict Mode double effects; flushSync."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"6.2. Stable in React 19 and later use; useOptimistic; useActionState (renamed from useFormState in the canaries); useFormStatus; form, button and input action props; async functions in startTransition; ref as a regular prop on function components and ref callback cleanup functions; rendering Context directly as a provider; document metadata hoisting (title, meta, link); stylesheet precedence and the preload, preinit, prefetchDNS and preconnect APIs; useDeferredValue initialValue; Server Components and Server Functions (stable as a protocol, requiring framework or bundler support); improved hydration mismatch diffs; forwardRef deprecated but functional; the immediate fallback commit and sibling prewarm behavior for Suspense."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"6.3. Stable in React 19.2 (October 2025; verify) Activity with mode visible or hidden; useEffectEvent; cacheSignal for server components; Performance tracks in browser devtools; batched Suspense reveals in streaming SSR; prerender and resume APIs for partial pre-rendering. React Compiler 1.0 was released separately in the same window and works with React 17 and later via a runtime shim."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"6.4. Experimental or canary only, not in any stable release at time of writing SuspenseList; the experimental taint APIs for server components; View Transitions integration (unstable_ViewTransition and addTransitionType, present in canaries through 2025, status in 19.2 stable uncertain, treat as experimental until confirmed); fragment refs; any unstable_ prefixed export."},
      {"kind":"p","text":"6.5. Removed or never shipped unstable_useContextSelector never shipped; ReactDOM.render, hydrate, unmountComponentNode and findDOMNode removed in 19; string refs, legacy context and propTypes on function components removed in 19; the Offscreen name replaced by Activity."},
    ],
  },
];;
